This library gives firmware a small, predictable building block: `MovingAverage<N>` (the template's example
utility) keeps the last samples in fixed storage and returns their mean in constant time. It needs no MCU, SDK or
RTOS: the same code runs on any microcontroller and on your PC, and every call that can fail says why.

## At a glance

| | |
| --- | --- |
| **What it is** | A platform-free C++20 library: `xmpl100::MovingAverage<N>` and `Result<T>` |
| **Does** | The mean of the last `N` samples in fixed storage, O(1) per sample; refuses NaN |
| **Library** | Header-only C++20; no heap, exceptions, RTTI or virtual calls; every fallible call returns `Result<T>` |
| **Runs on** | Any MCU with a C++20 compiler, and the host ([Platforms](docs/platforms.md)) |
| **Status** | 0.1.0, host-tested on GCC and Clang |

## Try it on your PC

[`examples/host`](examples/host/README.md) smooths a series of samples and prints the mean after each one.

```bash
cmake -S examples/host -B build-example && cmake --build build-example && ./build-example/xmpl100_host_example
```

